id: k8s.describe
kind: kubernetes
title: describe
aliases: kubectl describe, object inspection
tags: operations, diagnostics
questions:
  - where do you look first when a pod is not behaving?
invariant: describe renders the spec, the observed status and the recent events of one object in one place.
answer_30s: `kubectl describe` joins spec, status and events for one object, which is why it is the first command for almost any object-level problem.
answer_2m: |
  What to read, in order: Status and Conditions (Ready/ContainersReady), then
  the container status block (State, Last State, Reason like OOMKilled or
  ImagePullBackOff, Exit Code), then the probe configuration that caused
  failures, then Mounts and Environment (a missing ConfigMap key shows here),
  and finally Events. For a Service, describe shows the selector and the
  endpoints; for a PVC, the binding failure reason. It answers "what does the
  API server think the state of this object is" without YAML archaeology.
when_to_use:
  - first command for any object in the wrong state
commands:
  - kubectl describe pod api-... => probe failures, OOMKills, mount and scheduling errors in one page
  - kubectl describe node <node> => capacity, allocated requests, taints and evicted Pods
failure_modes:
  - reading only the pod's status and missing the event tail at the bottom
related:
  - k8s.events
  - k8s.debugging-workflow
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/generated/kubectl_describe/
